A||N||17||BMWTECH's Fiero GT||87 GT idling dilema||||5||
Z||000000||BMWTECH's Fiero GT||12-13-2001||09:17 PM||||This is kind of lenghty, so please bear with me. I have an 87 Gt that will not idle at the desired RPM.  I just rebuilt the 2.8, and installed a crane compucam cam kit. The car did not run when I got it, so I am not sure if this problem was there before the rebuild.  At cold start up the idle is around 1500RPM, and as it warms up the idle starts to seriously hunt.  It will fluctuate between 450-1200 rpm intermitently, but generally stay around 1000 RPM while hunting 200 rpm either direction. If left idling for more than 4 or 5 mins, the hunting becomes so severe it stalls. When put into gear it will bog down and almost stall, and then recover and start idling semi-normal while in gear.  Sometimes it will stall when dropped into gear...usually when hot.  Also when leaving a light or stop sign, the initial push on the accelerator will result in a hesitation followed by a strong take off. The car runs great off idle. The brand new catalytic convertor also smells as if the vehicle is running rich.  I have new cap, rotor, wires, spark plugs, alternator, battery, exhaust, and a set of used injectors that had aprox 10K miles on them before a friend totaled his 88 berretta with a 2.8.  Using a Snap On scanner, no codes are present.  O2 cross counts are good, coolant temp is good, MAT input is good, MAP vac fluctuates between 13-17"HG mostly follows idle RPM, Integrator is down around 83, with a block learn of 118.  I'm a tech, but I don't know a whole lot about GM products...I work on BMW'S.  Any insight you might have would be greatly appreciated, as this is driving me nuts!  Thanks in advance!||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-13-2001||10:32 PM||||Sounds like you've checked a lot of things, but did you check the timing?<P>Make sure to short out the two terminals in the ALDL (diagnostic connector) when reading/setting timing.  This will eliminate the computers control of timing, (Electronic Spark Timing)<P>Those sound like the symptoms to me.  Check the label on the decklid to verify what to time the car at, If you are going to be looking at the harmonic balancer its pretty much right above your head.  It also has the emissions info on it.<P>Check that timing, and then post your findings here.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||12-13-2001||11:45 PM||||BMW,  A couple of things to check...  I would check the voltage curve of the TPS per the desc of throttling from idle.  Also what are the IAC counts low & high as it is hunting?  If you didn't clean up the TB during the rebuild, did you clean the IAC passages through to the idle tube?||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-14-2001||06:27 PM||||83 and 118 means it's running seriously rich...im a GM tech.  Clean throttle body and iac motor and passages first and also check the timing.  Also, check to make sure you are not getting vac to egr valve at idle.  One more thing.  The neut safety switch being misadjusted can also cause the same hunting problem.  Check this last...experiment with moving it slightly at idle.  My bet is egr or timing prob........good luck.<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||BMWTECH's Fiero GT||12-15-2001||12:17 AM||||Wow...I actually got some replies!  This is the first fiero forum that has actually been some help....thank you.  I have checked the timing, set at 10 deg BTDC while in drive with the aldl grounded as desrcribed (actually set to field service mode with my snap on scanner which does the same thing.)  I have checked for vacuum leaks using the crude but effective method of spraying brake clean around possible leaks and observing how the engine responds.  EGR was my first guess also, so i blocked it off at the intake manifold for testing purposes, but it did not make a difference.  At idle my IAC counts are around 15-20, except when the cooling fan kicks on, then it jumps to around 60.  The idle actually fluctuates faster than the data transmission between the car and the scanner because the IAC counts move from 15 to possibly 25 while hunting.  I did clean up the throttle body and plate during overhaul, but did not remove the IAC and clean that passage...I will do this ASAP and hope it helps.  Also curious if any of you have had a pickup coil go bad, and what the effects were on the engine.  The reason I ask is I noticed the magnet crumbling and little pieces of it missing when I replaced the rotor.  I didn't think much about it at the time other than I would eventually have to replace it, but now I'm wondering if I am getting a week signal, which could be throwing off the spark advance and firing of the injectors.  Let me know what you guys think....and thank you VERY MUCH for taking the time to read and respond to this.  By the way I am a Mitsubishi master tech, and have been a BMW twch for a little over a year now, so if anyone has any problems in this area I can certianly be of some help....too bad I don't have just as much experience with GM!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||12-15-2001||02:37 AM||||You should probably set the base idle.<P>-Ground the ADL<BR>-Turn key ON<BR>-Listen to the IAC, let screw in for a minute<BR>-Unplug IAC<BR>-Turn key off<BR>-Unground ADL<BR>-Start car and get to idle at 500-600rpm by adjusting idle screw(may be plugged-use an awl and pop plug out)<BR>-Turn off car and reconnect IAC<BR>Start car...may take a try or two.<P>Use your scanner, make sure TPS voltage is .54<P>Not saying this is whats wrong, but it should be set anyway.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark Matthews||12-15-2001||09:26 AM||||Hey BMW, not sure if you know about it, but here's an awesome link for BMW forums.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.roadfly.com" TARGET=_blank>www.roadfly.com</A> <P>Sorry for the Off Topic!  I've got a '70 2002 that I mess around with.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top||||reg||1
Z||000007||BMWTECH's Fiero GT||12-15-2001||10:52 AM||||Hey thanks everyone!  I'll look into setting base idle as described, but I don't recall seeing the screw you are talking about...but I'll look.  I will also check tps. I think it was at .9v at idle which is in specs by the gm manual, but I will reset it to .54v and see what happens.  I'm going to clean the Iac passage, and replace the IAC. Also can't help but wonder if that pickup coil is causing problems, so that's getting replaced too.  Providing I can find the parts locally today, I'll try all of everyones suggestions and post the results...thanks again!||||reg||1
Z||000008||BMWTECH's Fiero GT||12-15-2001||10:54 AM||||Mark, thanks for the heads up on the roadfly site...I was not aware of it!  good luck with your 2002, we don't see to many of them!||||reg||1
Z||000009||GMGW3||12-15-2001||01:35 PM||||Well theres a couple of things that come to mind with that, It sure does sound like a sensor issue, but your saying your not getting any codes wich is odd. The sensors that I was thinking it might be are the MAP sensor or the throttle position sensor. If not, I saw similar symptoms with a 95 Eclipse wich it turned out to be a Bad EGR valve and EGR solenoid. Has all the EGR circuit been checked out? I know the EGR tubes crack and fail alot onthe V6, but usally also you would get a check engine light after its been running a bit. Only other thing I can think of is that Cam you are using, Im gussing its suppose to be ECM compatable since it is a compucam. You might also want to check the cam timing, or valve lash. I have had idle troubles from over tight valve rockers in the past, after the new lifters pump up with oil for the first time.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mark Matthews||12-15-2001||03:34 PM||||Sure BMW no worries.<P>Hey, my 87 GT is doing the exact same thing as yours,  and can't be tested for Echeck until it's fixed.  Lemme know what you find out!  I'm dying to get this thing on the roads.  I have my 10 day temp tag to get it fixed.<P>Hey BAM-BAM, give me a call when you get off work.  Maybe you can help me figure out whats wrong with mine.  Thanks!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top<p>[This message has been edited by Mark Matthews (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||BC-GT||12-16-2001||06:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BMWTECH's Fiero GT:<BR><B>  When put into gear it will bog down and almost stall, and then recover and start idling semi-normal while in gear.  Sometimes it will stall when dropped into gear...usually when hot.  Also when leaving a light or stop sign, the initial push on the accelerator will result in a hesitation followed by a strong take off. The car runs great off idle.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had the same problems described as above, not quite as severe, but identical, I've got a new 2.8 with all new sensors, as well. I just replaced me fuel pressure regulator and my hesitation went away. It still does *hunt* somewhat, usually only about + or -100 RPM though(acceptable to me)at this point in time with only 300 kilometres on it.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid27/pe8dae45bb8bf084e93628ae50c338631/fe1839db.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||12-16-2001||08:42 PM||||I've seen weak ignition coils and messed up pick-up coils do some strange things on V-6 Fiero's. I usually change pick-up coils every 3 years just to be safe. I have had 2 go bad. The first would do all kinds of wierd things like mess up the idle, Cause intermittent surges, Etc. The second one just died suddenly and shut down the car.<BR>To replace the pick-up coil, go to this link:<BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-pickupcoil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-pickupcoil.html</A> <P>Welcome to the forum!<BR>(Now I know someone I can ask BMW questions to when my wife gets her 325XI next month!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  )<P><P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mark Matthews||12-16-2001||10:50 PM||||Hmmm, I wonder if that's my problem, I'm pretty sure that's what the PO said he changed recently.  I wonder if it's bad, or maybe he didn't check the timing.  I wish I knew more about engines!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Guess that's why I'm here tho, to learn.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top||||reg||3
Z||000014||BMWTECH's Fiero GT||12-17-2001||12:44 PM||||Yesturday, I replaced the IAC, the distributer and the ign coil.  I removed the throttle body, cleaned all passages, and re installed.  While I had the intake off, I ran a quick compression check to verify proper valve lash, and cam timing.  All six cyl's were within 4psi of one another at 166-170psi.  I checked fuel press at idle, it was 42psi, which jumped to 49psi with the vac line to the regulator removed.  As far as I can tell, I have eliminated every possible cause, and still have the problem.  Addmittedly it is better, but still not right. O and I set the timing at 10 deg BTDC in drive, while warm with the aldl grounded.  I also reset the TPS per advice to .54V.  I have checked everything!  Here I am a well paid master mitsu tech, master ase tech, and level 3 BMW tech that can not figure out what is wrong with his own car!!!!!!  I have never had this much trouble fixing a driveability problem on anything!!  Hats off to the GM techs out there, you guys have your hands full if this typical of a GM product.  If anyone knows a pontiac tech, or are one yourself...PLEASE HELP!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000015||extinct||12-19-2001||02:03 AM||||Here are a few oddball questions for Joe T.<P>When setting the base idle on the v-6 does turning the srew in (clockwise) increase or decrease the base idle?<P>When you hook up a scanner (autoxray for example) does that gournd the ALDL?<P>Do you have to set the timing by using a timing lite, or can you use the reading off the scanner and turn the distr. until you get the proper setting on the scanner?<P><BR>Thanks Joe!!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by extinct (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||GTDude||12-19-2001||08:35 AM||||Only other thing I can think of, right off hand is the egr tube...mine was busted right where it goes in the intake manifold and I had the same prob cause you could not see it and it was just busted on one side so it felt  ok.  I am a Pontiac/GM tech btw.  Good luck!<P>phil pettus<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_3.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000017||Krashdavus||12-19-2001||11:15 AM||||I had a similar problem, but my idle never went low/or stalled.  The previous owner installed a new EGR tube (or had it installed) so I never looked there again...till I took the old rubber hose and listened closely to the base of the EGR, where the tube was bolted - godd*mn vaccuum leak!  Check the gasket for proper seating.  I spent the money on a new tube when all I need was a gasket.  Took me 6 months to find. Good luck.||||reg||1
